# THE MACHINE-LOCAL POINTER. `names.example.mjs` is its template; it resolves the `owner` / `peer`
# aliases the wiki uses to real display names, and points at the state directory. Every check imports
# from it rather than spelling a name - which is what makes the rest of this directory publishable as
# it stands, on a repository that is PUBLIC.
names.mjs

# Nothing else machine-local lives here: the account file, the two Chrome profiles, the verdict
# record, the debug APK and the phone baseline are OUTSIDE this repository, under `STATE_DIR`. The
# two rules below are therefore about a mistake rather than about normal operation - a file copied
# in "just to try something" is exactly how a credential reaches a public archive.
test-accounts.json
results.ndjson

# Scratch space for one-shot probes and triage scripts. A diagnosis usually needs a throwaway script
# written next to the rig it interrogates; before this directory existed they accumulated alongside
# the real checks until 285 of 362 files were residue and nobody could tell an instrument from a
# leftover. Write them here - nothing in it is ever committed, and it may be emptied without asking.
scratch/

# Run artefacts written next to the runner: captures, screenshots, logcat dumps.
*.log
*.png
logs/

# ...BUT `fixtures/` IS INPUT, NOT OUTPUT, AND THE RULE ABOVE SWALLOWED IT.
#
# `*.png` was written for `shot.mjs`, which drops `shot-W1.png` beside the runner and must never
# commit a screenshot of a logged-in client. It also matched `fixtures/msg4-image.png`, the picture
# MSG-4 uploads - so that fixture could not be committed, was absent on a machine rebuilt from a
# handoff bundle, and the check hung 30 s on an attachment stuck in `EN ATTENTE` before dying in a
# `until() timed out` that named neither the file nor the fact that it was missing (2026-09-04).
# A required input that the ignore rules make uncommittable is a fixture nobody can restore.
!fixtures/
!fixtures/*.png
